(#$comment #$enables-MakesComplCapable "#$enables-MakesComplCapable expresses the notion that an agent is
rendered completely capable (in the sense of #$capableOf) of doing
something by a certain state of affairs -- a situation, that is, or,
perhaps more frequently, an event. 'Being completely capable' implies that,
were one to attempt to do something, one would in fact succeed. More
accurately, (#$enables-MakesComplCapable CIRC SIT AGENT ACT_TYPE) is true
iff, in the circumstance represented by CIRC, SIT obtains, AGENT is
completely capable of performing actions of type ACT_TYPE in CIRC, and SIT
is in fact what causes AGENT to be completely capable of performing a token
of ACT_TYPE. An example: a military unit's clearing and securing a given
area makes another unit completely capable of advancing through that area;
short of extremely unlikely accident, the second unit would pass through,
were it to attempt. An example in which this predicate does NOT apply:
merely giving an ax to someone of ordinary physical build and skills does
not amount to enabling-MakesComplCapable him, for even with the ax he may
lack the strength, training, etc., necessary to ensure that he would
succeed were he to try. The 'enabling' in this case consists in the removal
of a preventing factor, namely the lack of a proper tool -- see
#$enables-RemovesDisablingFactors.") 
