(#$comment #$culturePrescribes-Categorical "(#$culturePrescribes-Categorical CULTURE ACTTYPE ROLE) 
  means that within CULTURE it is considered good or proper or 
  right to play ROLE in instances of ACTTYPE on a regular basis,
  and bad or improper or wrong not to do so.  For example, some
  cultures consider it proper to make an offering to a deity each day.  
  This does not entail, however, that CULTURE has a (hypothetical) 
  prescription for playing ROLE in instances of ACTTYPE with respect 
  to every possible condition; i.e. there might exist exceptional 
  situations in which playing ROLE in an ACTTYPE is not prescribed 
  (and might even be proscribed) by CULTURE.  One might be temporarily
  exempted from the daily religious offering duty, for example, while 
  engaged in a protracted military battle.  This predicate applies 
  equally to moral rules, rules of conduct or etiquette, and customs.  
  See also #$culturePrescribes-Hypothetical, 
  #$cultureProscribes-Categorical, and #$cultureProscribes-Hypothetical.") 
