(#$comment #$enables-RemovesContraryFactors "#$enables-RemovesContraryFactors expresses the notion that an
agent may be 'enabled' by an event in the following sense: the event
removes all factors that are 'contrary', i.e., that would decrease the
likelihood of the agent's success -- put another way: all factors that
would make it less likely that the agent would succeed in performing the
action if she tried (see #$isAContraryFactorFor). More accurately, (#$enables-RemovesContraryFactors CIRC
SIT AGENT ACT_TYPE) is true iff, in the circumstance represented by CIRC,
SIT obtains, there are no contrary factors for AGENT to do an instance of
ACT_TYPE, and SIT is the cause of there being no such contrary
factors. Hence #$enables-RemovesContraryFactors does not imply that the
agent is in fact capable of doing an action: rather, it entails that, given
SIT, the probability of the agent's success is increased. Example: proper
bicycle maintenance enables the cyclist to complete the race.") 
