(#$comment #$DateInSituationFn "Similar to #$DayFn, #$MonthFn, etc.,
#$DateInSituationFn returns the specified day or month or year, etc., of some event or temporal situation.  In contrast to #$DayFn et al., #$DateInSituationFn is ternary; its first argument specifies the type of calendar interval (day, month, etc.) being denoted.  Thus, (#$DateInSituationFn DATE-TYPE N SIT) returns the Nth DATE-TYPE of the temporal situation SIT.  E.g., to refer to the 24th day of the Iranian hostage crisis, we can use (#$DateInSituationFn #$CalendarDay 24 #$IranHostageCrisis).") 
