(#$comment #$enables-RemovesDisablingFactors "#$enables-RemovesDisablingFactors expresses the notion that an
agent may be 'enabled' by an event in the following sense: the event
removes all factors that are 'disabling', i.e., that would cause the agent
not to be capable of doing a given action -- put another way: all factors
that would prevent the agent from performing the action. More accurately,
(#$enables-RemovesDisablingFactors CIRC SIT AGENT ACT_TYPE) is true iff, in
the circumstance represented by CIRC, SIT obtains, there are no factors
preventing AGENT from performing an instance of ACT_TYPE, and SIT is the
cause of there being no such disabling factors. Hence
#$enables-RemovesDisablingFactors does not imply that the agent is in fact
capable of doing a token of ACT_TYPE: it merely entails that no factor that
would disable her is present. Note that in general this is a stronger
notion than merely removing incapacitating factors. Example: giving an ax
or a saw to a physically fit adult individual would typically enable him to
chop down a tree in this sense, since in typical circumstances not having
an ax would be disabling -- though perhaps not incapacitating. Complex
events such as military tasks may also be regarded as typical examples of
this concept -- e.g., in a #$ReliefInPlace-MilitaryTask the relieved unit
is enabled in this sense to disengage and perform service support tasks.") 
