(#$cyclistNotes #$Bug-Software "Karen has made the following observation about #$Bug-Software: If flaws are represented as propositions, then a flaw of a piece of software is probably not equal to the flaw of the running version.  For a running version, the flaw might be the proposition that says 'when file xyz does not exist, an error break occurs when the user tries to access it'.  The software version of the flaw would be the proposition that says 'when this program is run, and file xyz does not exist, an error break occurs when the user tries to access it'. I agree -- but think it is possible to keep a neutral stance on this and just assume that a bug is a design flaw that affects both the program and its running version. The ontological effects are different, but we can always tell what is meant by checking the type of the first arg to #$flaws. The second argument to #$flaws is of type #$Flaw, a reified notion rather than a proposition. The propositional counterpart of a #$Flaw can be derived from additional axioms, which use the flaw context to derive an accurate proposition.") 
