(#$comment #$TestQuestionMicrotheory "A collection of microtheories. Each instance corresponds to a particular KE test, i.e. it is primarily meant to check or verify some property of the knowledge base, although this will often depend on code support. 

Each #$TestQuestionMicrotheory allows one to optionally specify a scenario in which one tries to prove a query. This is done with #$testScenarioEnglish and #$testScenarioCycL.  In certain rare cases one may only wish to setup a 
scenario and not associate any particular question with that particular #$TestQuestionMicrotheory.  In these cases, one may often have other #$TestQuestionMicrotheorys which have the #$TestQuestionMicrotheory in question as their #$genlMt.

Aside from the above mentioned exceptions, each #$TestQuestionMicrotheory is associated with a particular question specified by #$testQuestionEnglish and #$testQuestionCycL.

Answers to test questions are specified by #$testAnswersEnglish and #$testAnswersCycL.

Given (#$testQuestionCycL TEST QUESTION) and (#$testAnswersCycL TEST ANSWERS) as well as optional resource specifiers such as (#$testBackchain TEST BACKCHAIN), (#$testNumber TEST NUMBER) etc..,  a test is said to be ``working correctly'' (see also #$Hit-Exact) such that when the function ASK is called on QUESTION subject to any specified resources it returns bindings which appropriately match ANSWERS.  See #$testAnswersCycL for more on what ``appropriately match'' means.

Given (#$testCyclistsResponsible TEST CYCLIST), CYCLIST may record what they believe to the the current status (see #$TestStatusAttribute) on #$testStatus-Manual.  Cyclists may also consult #$testStatus-Automatic for status information about recent batch tests involving TEST.") 
