(#$comment #$antiSymptomOfMalfunction "(#$antiSymptomOfMalfunction ANTISYMPTOM MALFUNCTION) means that the presence of an instance of ANTISYMPTOM is counterindicative of the presence of an instance of MALFUNCTION  within the relevant system.  That is, everything else being equal, the presence of an instance of the former makes the presence of  an instance of the latter less likely than it otherwise would be. For instance, a car engine's having a high idle speed is an anti- symptom for the car's being out of gas.  Note that while  (#$antiSymptomOfMalfunction ANTISYMPTOM MALFUNCTION) entails  (#not (#symptomOfMalfunction ANTISYMPTOM MALFUNCTION)), the reverse entailment fails.  The predicate is intended to relate  certain symptoms to kinds of malfunctions that they rule out. A nonworking car radio, for example, is neither a symptom nor an  antisymptom of an empty gas tank, because the presence of the former  condition makes the presence of the latter neither more nor less  probable than it otherwise would be.") 
