(#$comment #$Function-Denotational "#$Function-Denotational is the collection of functions in the Cyc language whose uses denote terms, and are to be treated as NATs (non-atomic terms).  In other words, these functions, when applied to arguments of the correct number and type, form new terms that may then freely appear as arguments in other CycL expressions.  For example, consider #$GovernmentFn.  It takes one argument, such as #$France, and the resulting NAT --- in this case (#$GovernmentFn #$France) --- can be used anywhere in CycL expressions that an instance of #$RegionalGovernment could occur.  We could, instead, have created a specific new term `GovernmentOfFrance', asserted that it #$isa #$RegionalGovernment, etc., but by having NATs we can drastically reduce the total size of the Cyc vocabulary of constant terms.  
This collection does not include: 
1) abstract functions, like the one-to-one function that must exist between the positive integers and the rational numbers (see #$FunctionTheMathematicalType for such functions);
2) Predicates and other #$TruthFunctions.  #$Predicates, #$Quantifiers and #$LogicalConnectives can be thought of as functions from a set of arguments to a truth value.  Formulas that start with a #$TruthFunction can even serve as terms, i.e., as arguments to other predicates, but when they do so they can't just be replaced with their truth value!  So #$TruthFunction is not a subset of #$Function-Denotational.") 
