(#$comment #$UsingAForFn "This function can be used to denote the collection of instances of #$Event in
which some instance of #$Agent deliberately uses some instance of
a subcollection of #$SomethingExisting in some subcollection of #$PurposefulAction.
(#$UsingAForFn OBJ_TYPE ACT_TYPE ROLE) denotes the subcollection of 
#$PurposefulAction in which an instance OBJ of the  subcollection OBJ_TYPE
of #$SomethingExisting is used in an instance ACT of some subcollection ACT_TYPE of 
#$PurposefulAction in the instance ROLE of #$Role. For example, 
(#$UsingAForFn #$Hammer #$ConstructingABuilding #$deviceUsed) denotes the subcollection
of #$PurposefulAction in which an instance of #$Hammer is used in
an instance of #$ConstructingABuilding as a #$deviceUsed.
(#$UsingAForFn #$Recipe-CW #$CookingFood #$instructionsFollowed) denotes
the subcollection of #$PurposefulAction in which an instance of
#$Recipe-CW is used in an instance of #$CookingFood as the
#$instructionsFollowed.  Note that ACT_TYPE need not be the 
#$typePrimaryFunction for OBJ_TYPE, e.g. one could in principle
reify (#$UsingAForFn #$HuntingKnife #$Whittling #$deviceUsed), though
#$Whittling is not a #$typePrimaryFunction of #$HuntingKnife.") 
(#$comment #$AvenueOfApproachSystem "A collection of networks of instances of #$AvenueOfApproach:
more specifically, a collection of #$PathSystems such that all of the
paths composing each instance are themselves instances of
#$AvenueOfApproach.  Such networks can include 'loops' and are not
necessarily connected in the sense that, for any two points in the
system, there must exist an #$AvenueOfApproach between them. See the
definition of #$PathSystem for more details.  See also
#$pointInSystem, #$nodeInSystem, #$linkInSystem,
#$linkBetweenInSystem, #$pathInSystem, #$pathBetweenInSystem,
#$pointOnPath, #$betweenOnPath, #$loopInSystem, #$pointOnCycle,
#$junctionInSystem, #$deadEndInSystem, #$isolatedNodeInSystem and
#$connectedInSystem.") 
