(#$comment #$reasonForAction "(#$reasonForAction ACTION PRED AGENT THING) means 
that ACTION was performed by AGENT, and that a crucial reason for AGENT's 
performing ACTION is the fact that (PRED AGENT THING), where PRED is an instance 
of #$MentalSlot. For example, if the Saudi Arabian air force were 
to conduct a  bombing raid (RAID01) against targets in Iran shortly after the 
occurence of a terrorist attack (ATTACK01) directed against Saudi Arabia, and 
believed by the Saudis to have been sponsored by Iran, then it is reasonable to 
conclude that (#$reasonForAction RAID01 #$beliefs #$SaudiArabia 
(#$directingAgent ATTACK01 #$Iran)). Similarly, if Joe-Bob jumps (JUMP01) up 
from his desk because he sees a spider (SPIDER01) on it, then it is true that 
(#$reasonForAction JUMP01 #$sees Joe-Bob SPIDER01). When arg2 is an instance of 
PropositionalAttitudeSlot, arg4 will generally be a CycFormula. However, as the 
example of Joe-Bob and the spider shows, it need not always be a CycFormula.

Note: #$reasonForAction is not a spec-pred of #$inReactionTo, but in 
some cases a given claim can be expressed using either of these predicates. For 
example, the sentence above explaining Saudi Arabia's motivation for a 
hypothetical attack on Iran might also be expressed as: (#$inReactionTo RAID01 
ATTACK01). However, #$reasonForAction is a more discriminating predicate, in 
that it allows one to identify the precise attitude that motivated a given 
action. For this reason, #$reasonForAction is somewhat less versatile, since one 
must have more detailed information about the agent's mental state to use this 
predicate than to use #$inReactionTo; on the other hand, when this information 
is available, it can be quite useful to make it explicit. Finally, note that in 
some cases #$reasonForAction is more versatile than #$inReactionTo for instance, 
#$inReactionTo does not apply to the example above involving Joe-Bob and the 
spider. See the comment on #$inReactionTo.") 
