(#$cyclistNotes #$UsingAForFn "Where (#$UsingAForFn OBJ_TYPE ACT_TYPE ROLE) = USE_TYPE, 
there are at least two distinct relations that commonly exist between
USE_TYPE and ACT_TYPE.  First, USE_TYPE will in many cases be a
subcollection of ACT_TYPE.  For example, (#$UsingAForFn #$Hammer 
#$PryingSomething #$deviceUsed) is (plausibly) a subcollection of
#$PryingSomething.  Second, USE_TYPE will in other cases be a
collection of #$subEvents of instances of ACT_TYPE.  For example,
(#$UsingAForFn #$Hammer #$ConstructingABuilding #$deviceUsed) is not
a subcollection of #$ConstructingABuilding, since an action in which 
a hammer is used in constructing a building is not itself the 
construction of the entire building; instead, the hammer-using action
is a subevent of a building-construction event.  It is unclear at this 
point whether or not it is possible to state general rules for when the
first relation above holds between USE_TYPE and ACT_TYPE versus when
the second relation holds, or even whether or not there are still other
relations that commonly hold between USE_TYPE and ACT_TYPE.  But we can
at least say this: the simpler and more specific ACT_TYPE is, the more
likely the first (#$genls) relation holds.") 
