(#$comment #$assertionFormula "(#$assertionFormula ASSERTION EL-FORMULA) means that ASSERTION (at least partially) represents the
epistemological formula EL-FORMULA.  Note that each assertion (see
#$Assertion) will have exactly one corresponding epistemological
formula.  However, some formulas may correspond to more than one assertion.
For example, the formula (#$isa #$Muffet #$Poodle) can correspond to several distinct assertions.  For example, these two assertions (#$ist #$SportsMt (#$isa #$Muffet #$Poodle)) and (#$ist #$CyclistsMt (#$isa #$Muffet #$Poodle)) contain the formula (#$isa #$Muffet #$Poodle).  Anytime a formula is asserted into multiple microtheories, the resulting assertions in each of those microtheories will share the common formula.  Another way that a fomula may correspond to multiple assertions is if it poly-canonicalizes.  An example of such an assertion can be seen in #$MetaAssertionsForPolyCanonicalizingAssertions.") 
