(#$comment #$motionPathway-Complete "The predicate #$motionPathway-Complete is used to indicate that the whole #$Trajectory (i.e., the #$trajectory-Complete) travelled by a moving object in a particular event follows a certain #$Path-Spatial along its whole length, and that the movement goes along the whole of the #$Path-Spatial from end to end.  (#$motionPathway-Complete MOVE PATH) means that the #$Path-Spatial PATH contains the entire #$Trajectory along which an #$objectMoving travels in the #$Movement-TranslationEvent MOVE.  (The #$Traversal determined by #$trajectory-Complete occupies the whole length of PATH.)  There can be at most one #$motionPathway-Complete if MOVE is an element of #$Translation-SingleTrajectory.  If MOVE is an element of #$Translation-MultiTrajectory, then there can be two or more #$motionPathway-Completes; i.e., at least two distinct values of PATH such that (#$motionPathway-Complete MOVE PATH) is true.  PATH may be a marked or unmarked #$Path-Spatial.  By definition, any #$motionPathway-Complete of a #$Movement-TranslationEvent connects the starting and stopping points (#$fromLocation, #$toLocation) of the whole movement event.  (See also #$pathConnects.)  Use #$trajectoryPassesThrough to state that an object passes through a particular location on the #$Trajectory.   Note:  Compare #$motionPathway-Complete with #$motionPathway-Partial; the former refers to a #$Path-Spatial containing the entire route of a particular #$trajectory-Complete.  A sub-region (shorter segment) of a #$motionPathway-Complete is NOT itself considered a #$motionPathway-Complete of the same movement event; i.e., if (#$motionPathway-Complete MOVE PATH) is true, and we cut PATH up into ten pieces PATH1, ..., PATH10, then it will generally not be true that (#$motionPathway-Complete MOVE PATH8),  but it will be true that (#$motionPathway-Partial MOVE PATH8).") 
