(#$comment #$ComputerProcess-Generic "The collection of events in which a 'process' is executed on a
#$ComputationalSystem, using the #$ComputationalSystem's resources.
'Process' here refers to the act of a CPU or multiple CPUs executing the 
instructions in a #$ComputerProgramCopy.  A process ends when
it is terminated (by its own instructions, the operating
system, a power outage, etc.)
A #$ComputerProcess-Generic can be suspended or swapped out by
the #$OperatingSystem (whereupon it temporarily ceases to exist)
but when it is swapped back in,
it will still be the same #$ComputerProcess-Generic.
Hence, the time during which a #$ComputerProcess-Generic exists can
be a discontinuous time interval.
#$ComputerProcess-Generics can exist on multiple #$Computers
(e.g. a single instance of #$ComputerProcess-Generic distributed over a network),
but cease to exist on a #$Computer when that #$Computer is rebooted.
Note that this is slightly more general than the notion of process
in standard computer terminology, e.g. 'a Unix process', which corresponds
more closely with #$ComputerProcess-SingleComputer.") 
