(#$comment #$SpatialThing-Localized "This is the collection of all spatial things that can be meaningfully said to have location or position in the empirically observable Universe of the context in which the term in question is defined.  It includes both #$PartiallyTangible entities like pyramids and ships, and #$Intangible entities like the #$Equator.  The collection itself is agnostic with respect to the tangibility of its instances, although it is presumably inclusive of all #$PartiallyTangible entities.  #$Events that can be pinned down to specific places, such as #$PhysicalEvents, are also instances of #$SpatialThing-Localized.  However, many #$Events are non-examples; e.g., a law coming into effect.  Mental events are probably not examples--at least not in every #$Microtheory. Possible case of #$SpatialThing which are not cases #$SpatialThing-Localized: certain instances of #$TheTerm.  For example, if someone were to create (#$The #$Circle).  Also, all instances of #$SpatialThing-Localized have a temporally finite lifespan, the upper bound being the lifespan of space-time, or the lifespan of the space-time of the context of definition. Imaginary entities like Frodo, Captain Queeg, and #$HAL9000-TheComputer may be localized within the imaginary universes attaching the fictitious contexts in which they are defined, and so may be instances of #$SpatialThing-Localized within those microtheoretic contexts.") 
