(#$comment #$genericPathway-Complete "The predicate #$genericPathway-Complete is used to indicate that the           #$transferredThing for some instance of #$GeneralizedTransfer follows some           instance of #$Path-Spatial throughout the entire transfer.           (#$genericPathway-Complete TRANS PATH) means that the entire route of TRANS           is spatial and follows PATH.  By definition, any #$genericPathway-Complete of an instance of #$GeneralizedTransfer
          connects the 'starting' and 'stopping' points, the #$from-Generic and the 
          #$to-Generic, of the entire instance.  Note: Compare #$genericPathway-Complete with
          #$genericPathway-Partial: the former refers to an instance of #$Path-Spatial
          containing the entire route of a particular #$GeneralizedTransfer.  A sub-region
          (shorter segment) of a #$genericPathway-Complete is NOT itself considered a
          #$genericPathway-Complete of the same #$GeneralizedTransfer, i.e. 
          if (#$genericPathway-Complete TRANS PATH) is true and we cut PATH up into ten
          pieces PATH1, ..., PATH10, then it will generally not be true that
          (#$genericPathway-Complete MOVE PATH8), but it will be true that
          (#$genericPathway-Partial MOVE PATH8).  See also #$infoPathway-Partial and #$motionPathway-Partial.  Note that it is usually better to use one of these more specific predicates.") 
