(#$comment #$pathInSystem "(#$pathInSystem PATH SYS) means that PATH is a path in the path system SYS.  Paths in SYS are defined as follows.  (i) Every link in SYS is a path in SYS.  (ii) Every subpath between 'points' on a link in SYS is a path in SYS.  (iii) The concatenation of paths PATH1 and PATH2 in SYS is a path in SYS, provided there is only one point on both paths.  Every path in the system is constructible by applying (i), (ii) and/or (iii) finitely many times.  So a path in SYS can 'start' somewhere along a link, 'go through' several other links and nodes, and 'end' somewhere along a link (note that the path may be undirected).  Or it could just go from node to node.  This is defined precisely, using #$SubPathBetweenFn, #$JoinPathsFn, #$pathBetweenInSystem and #$linkBetweenInSystem.") 
