(#$comment #$testAnswersCycL "A test, by definition, involves comparing the results with some expectation or ''correct answer''.  This is encoded by (#$testAnswersCycL MT LIST) where LIST is a list of bindings that we expect to be returned by calling fi-ask or the HTML Ask Interface on the #$testQuestionCycL.  The syntax of values for LIST is similar to that for binding lists which ask returns except that, due to some current (Nov 98) peculiar code limitations, the period characters must be removed.  Thus, if the target answer for FooTestMt is 'true' then one should assert (#$testAnswersCycL FooTestMt (((T T)))).  An exception to this syntax occurs if the target answer is NIL for FooTestMt.  In this case one should assert (#$testAnswersCycL FootTestMt ((nil))).  Note that the list is not necessarily complete (more may be returned) but it is a minimum.  If more are returned, they are currently (April '98) considered false positives (see #$FalsePositives).  If a human sees that a test for which they are responsible is getting false positives, then they  should decide whether a given false positive is correct (in which case it should be added as a new #$testAnswersCycL) or incorrect.") 
