(#$comment #$posesGreaterVulnerabilityTo-Object "(#$posesGreaterVulnerabilityTo-Object OBJ TARGET1 TARGET2 HARM-TYPE) means that, all other things being equal, OBJ poses a greater potential harm of HARM-TYPE to TARGET1 than to TARGET2.  By implication this means that TARGET1 is at least vulnerable to OBJ (although TARGET2 may not be).  This is one of several comparative vulnerability predicates.  #$posesGreaterVulnerabilityThan-Object differs from #$makesMoreVulnerableThan-Object in that the former expresses a comparative difference in the things threatened (TARGET1 is more vulnerable than TARGET2), whereas the latter expresses a comparative difference in the threats.  As a rule you should use #$posesGreaterVulnerabilityThan-Object when comparing how one object threatens two distinct targets; use #$makesMoreVulnerableThan-Object when comparing two threats to one object.  See #$NoteAboutVulnerability for more on these distinctions.") 
