(#$comment #$NoteAboutElectronicAttacks "What in English are called 'cyber attacks' or 'electronic attacks' are represented in Cyc by constants that are specs of #$AttackByComputerOperation. There are numerous subcollections of #$AttackByComputerOperation.  Examples of specs of #$AttackByComputerOperation include #$ElectronicAttack-DenialOfService,  #$ElectronicAttack-Virus,
#$ElectronicAttack-Coordinated, and others. All specs of
#$AttackByComputerOperation are asserted to be instances of
#$ElectronicAttackType (to be discussed below),  and may be instances
of its specs.

Currently (as of 5/2/2000), there are four notable subcollections of
#$ElectronicAttackType.   They are: #$ElectronicAttackTypeByProgram,
#$ElectronicAttackTypeByMethod,  #$ElectronicAttackTypeByPurpose, and
#$ElectronicAttackTypeByDegreeOfSecrecy.

Consider some examples. The collection #$ElectronicAttack-Virus is an
instance  of #$ElectronicAttackTypeByProgram, a spec of
#$AttackByComputerOperation, and its instances are attacks using a computer
virus program.
#$ElectronicAttackTypeByMethod, a spec of #$AttackByComputerOperation, and has
instances that  are distributed attacks.

The vocabulary used to name constants in the electronic attack
hierarchy usually has the format '#$ElectronicAttack-x',  where 'x'
refers to a conventional name of an attack (such as 'denial of
sevice').  Each of these collections  is a spec of #$AttackByComputerOperation,
and each is an instance of some type level collection in the
electronic attack hierarchy.

Some constants in the electronic attack hierarchy have the format
'#$ElectronicIntelligenceAttack-x'.  These constants designate specs
of  #$ElectronicIntelligenceAttack-General, which itself is a spec of
#$AttackByComputerOperation,  and an instance of #$ElectronicAttackType.") 
