(#$cyclistNotes #$vulnerabilityCascades "Those familiar with the vulnerability vocabulary will notice that this one predicate is intended to cover both object and situational vulnerabilities.  While this may seem sloppy and perhaps reckless, it has two advantages and few drawbacks.  First, it allows us to use one predicate to cover both types of vulnerabilities.  The property that some vulnerabilities cascade (as I have termed it) applies equally to object and situational vulnerabilities.  Second, it allows us to draw a broad range of vulnerability judgments on the basis of one GAF.  The clear drawbacks to #$vulnerabilityCascades are manageable given the following restrictions on rules.  Rarely if ever should a rule conclude a #$vulnerabilityCascades GAF.  The reason is that the relationship among the arguments is very complex, and perhaps too difficult to represent in Cyc at this point.  The second is that rules with this predicate in the antecedant will also contain a GAF that restricts the vulnerability in question to an object or situational vulnerability.  Because of this added GAF the paramaters on #$vulnerabilityCascades may be broad; no harm will result .") 
