(#$comment #$inRegion "(#$inRegion OBJ1 OBJ2) means that, roughly speaking, OBJ1 is 'at'
OBJ2.  #$inRegion is a general locative predicate which has a large,
diverse group of specializations; for this reason it is hard to
characterize its meaning succinctly.  The primary use of #$inRegion is
to generalize over all these specializations.  The sufficient
conditions for #$inRegion are the disjunction of the sufficient
conditions for its specializations (but see EXCEPTIONS).  Every
assertion in the extent of #$inRegion is, in theory, in the extent of
at least one of its specializations (again, see EXCEPTIONS).  When
considering using #$inRegion, it is always best to first consult its
specializations -- particularly when maximum specificity is important,
e.g. when the considered usage is a GAF or the consequent of a rule.
By contrast, if one wishes to write a very general antecedent with a
locative predicate, #$inRegion is more likely to be appropriate.  Two
important immediate specializations of #$inRegion are
#$objectFoundInLocation, whose arg2 is necessarily not a part of arg1,
and #$physicalDecompositions, whose arg1 necessarily *is* a part of
arg2 (note the switch of argument order; #$physicalDecompositions is a
value on #$genlInverse, not on #$genlPreds).  #$spatiallySubsumes is
another important immediate specialization.  Some examples: Given that
Cup1 is on Stool5, we may write (#$inRegion Cup1 Stool5).  Note,
however, that #$on-Physical is probably a more appropriately precise
relation.  If Cup1 were under the stool, (#$inRegion Cup1 Stool5) would
probably be false.  Likewise if Cup1 were suspended 5 centimeters
above the stool then (#$inRegion Cup1 Stool5) would be false.  On the
other hand, if Cup1 were on a 5 centimeter high stack of papers, it
would be consistent to assert (#$inRegion Cup1 Stool5) because
(#$objectFoundInLocation Cup1 Stool5) is true.  Given that Fish1 is in
Ocean2 we may write (#$inRegion Fish1 Ocean2).  (But
#$in-ImmersedFully is probably more precise.)  About Car03 on Highway2
we may write (#$inRegion Car03 Highway2), although #$on-Physical is a
more precise rendering.  If Joe is in Texas we may write (#$inRegion
Joe Texas) although #$objectFoundInLocation is more appropriately
precise.  Given a gorge which has a cliff as a part, one may write
(#$inRegion Cliff01 Gorge01) although (#$surfaceParts Gorge01 Cliff01
would be a more precise rendering.  EXCEPTIONS: The cavities denoted
(#$InteriorFn OBJ) may or may not be a part of OBJ depending on one's
viewpoint.  To accommodate both the affirmative and negative answers
to this question we shall conclude (#$inRegion (#$InteriorFn OBJ) OBJ)
which is agnostic as to whether (#$InteriorFn OBJ) is or is not a part
of OBJ.  More specialized contexts may use more specific predicates
(e.g. #$objectFoundInLocation vs #$physicalParts) for the relationship
between e.g. a sponge and the holes in a sponge.  There are currently
(Jan 98) no other exceptions.  As they may acrete, it will be
important to periodically re-examine the rules on #$inRegion to
determine if a new specialization of it should be introduced.") 
