(#$comment #$NoteAboutMarkupLanguages "There is a more basic comment on the nature of markup languages in the  comment for #$MarkupLanguage.  Note that there are different ways to represent markup languages.  The issue is that some markup languages are based on others.  It is not clear whether this is a genls-spec relation. For instance, #$XML-BasedLanguage is a spec of #$MarkupLanguage and #$XML-MarkupLanguage is an instance of it.  However, some might argue that #$XML-BasedLanguage should be a spec of #$XML-MarkupLanguage which should, in turn, be a spec of #$MarkupLanguage.  Nevertheless, #$XML-MarkupLanguage has been left as a specific language rather than a language type. The analogy here might be between English and some regional dialect of it.  Whether or not dialects that have their basis in English are specs or instances of English is unclear.  To note that a language is based upon another language is not necessarily to say that it is an example or instance of it.") 
