(#$comment #$rateOfEventType-Place "A predicate useful for representing the rates or frequencies at which various types of           events occur in various regions or places without having to specify the actors           in the event types.  (#$rateOfEventType-Place PLACE EVENT_TYPE NUM TIME_QUANTITY)           means that at the instance of #$Place PLACE, events of the type EVENT_TYPE           occur at a rate of NUM per TIME_QUANTITY.   For example, if vandalism events           occur at an average rate of 3.2 per month (averaged over a year, say)            in a particular neighborhood NEIGHBORHOOD, we can represent this as           (#$rateOfEventType-Place NEIGHBORHOOD #$Vandalism           3.2 (#$MonthsDuration 1)).  Normally, one will want to qualify GAFS using this           predicate with #$holdsIn, especially when representing average rates.  For instance,           in the example above, one would temporally qualify the GAF using the year over           which the average is taken, e.g. (#$holdsIn 1995 (#$rateOfEventType-Place NEIGHBORHOOD           #$Vandalism 3.2 (#$MonthsDuration 1))), if the average was taken over the year 1995.           Even when not representing rates resulting from taking some average, one may           want to temporally qualify GAFS using this predicate.  E.g., if in the            #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica, national elections occurred at a rate of once every           two years throughout the twentieth century, one could represent this by           (#$holdsIn #$TheTwentiethCenturyAD (#$rateOfEventType-Place #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica           HOLDING_NATIONAL_ELECTION 1 (YearsDuration 2))).") 
