(#$comment #$longitude "(#$longitude THING ANGULAR-DISTANCE) means that the #$SpatialThing THING is located at the east longitude ANGULAR-DISTANCE, in the sense that either the whole of THING lies at this longitude or THING has a proper part which lies at this longitude.  Thus if THING is, for instance, a large geographical region, there can be more than one true assertion of the form (#$longitude THING X).  Some examples of assertions about longitude are:  (#$longitude #$PrimeMeridian (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 0)) and (#$longitude #$CityOfAustinTX (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure -97.7)).  As the last example indicates, longitudes of things in the western hemisphere are expressed using negative real numbers.  We adopt the convention that the the longitude of the north and south poles is 0 degrees.") 
