(#$comment #$ComprehensiveTestBanTreaty "A treaty designed to institute a virtual world-wide ban on the testing
  of nuclear weapons.  The #$ComprehensiveTestBanTreaty (CTBT) prohibits all
  #$agreeingAgents from conducting any nuclear explosive test, or permitting such 
  a test to be performed on territories under their control, and it establishes
  an international monitoring system to verify compliance.  It was approved by
  the United Nations General Assembly in September 1996.  Its text specifically
  names 44 states now possessing significant civilian and/or military nuclear
  capabilities that must ratify the treaty before it can enter into force.
  As of February 1999, 152 nations have signed, including all five nuclear-weapon
  states (i.e. the United States, Russia, Britain, France and China) and 29,
  including France and the United Kingdom (but not the US), have ratified the
  treaty.  The US transmitted the CTBT to the Senate in September 1997 for its
  advice and consent to ratification.  President Clinton has called on the Senate
  to approve the Treaty this year (1999).") 
