(#$cyclistNotes #$makesMoreVulnerableThan-Situation "There is a strong argument to be made for dropping the fourth argument so that we can represent a vast range of knowledge about comparative vulnerabilities without being hindered by expressions of hardship result embodied in the fourth argument.  Precision wins out, however, and we currently require that comparative vulnerability judgments say something about the hardship results.  Although this will initially limit the range of GAFs we can produce with this predicate, it will prevent rampant production of nonsense GAFs in which two entirely unrelated situations get compared in terms of their threat to OBJ.  Ultimately we will write rules to relate the various result types so that the KB will contain a wide range of systematic vulnerability assessments using this predicate (4/2000 - #$DeSanto)") 
