(#$comment #$programAlgorithms "This predicate relates code for a computer
  program to an 
  algorithm used by that code.  (#$programAlgorithms CODE ALGORITHM) 
  means that CODE uses ALGORITHM: the event of running a copy of CODE
  on a computer system involves the carrying out of ALGORITHM on one
  or more inputs (see #$computerProcessAlgorithms).  ALGORITHM might 
  correspond to part or all of CODE; in the former case ALGORITHM 
  will be a proper #$subAlgorithms of the ''maximal'' algorithm 
  (see #$programMaxAlgorithm) used by CODE.  Different computer code, 
  whether written in the same or in different languages, can use 
  the same algorithm; and if the codes differ only in ways that are 
  irrelevent to the (entire) sequence of operations that they 
  represent, they will use all of the same algorithms.  Two versions 
  of code for the same instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW will not 
  necessarily use all (or even many) of the same algorithms; and the 
  algorithms used by one of these versions might not all even be 
  functionally equivalent (see #$equivAlgorithms) to their counterparts 
  used by the other version, if (say) some of the program's functionality 
  changes from version to version (e.g. WordPerfect 5.1 versus 
  WordPerfect 6.0).") 
