(#$comment #$latitude "(#$latitude THING ANGULAR-DISTANCE) means that the #$SpatialThing THING is located at the north latitude ANGULAR-DISTANCE, in the sense that either the whole of THING lies at this latitude or THING has a proper part which lies at this latitude.  Thus if THING is, for instance, a large geographical region, there can be more than one true assertion of the form (#$latitude THING X).  Some examples of assertions about latitude are:  (#$latitude #$Equator (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 0)), (#$latitude #$NorthPole (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 90)), and (#$latitude #$AntarcticCircle (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure -66.5)).  As the last example indicates, latitudes of things in the southern hemisphere are expressed using negative real numbers.") 
