(#$comment #$ProtestantPerson "The collection of people who adhere to a faith in
#$ProtestantReligion. The basic tenet of Protestantism is the non-mandatory
role of the #$Priest as an intercessionary between a believer and #$GodOfAbrahamIsaacAndJacob,
or between a believer and the proper interpretation of the holy texts (such as
the #$Bible). As a divergence from #$Catholicism, Protestants generally believe in
a school of Christianity that differs in some key
respects. Among the chief differences are: #$CatholicPerson shows more devotion
to #$Mary-MotherOfJesus, and has a belief in
#$Transubstantiation -- the process whereby bread is turned into the body of 
#$JesusChrist). Some subclasses of #$ProtestantPerson view the latter as being
of symbolic import only (e.g., #$AnglicanReligion), while followers of the #$Lutheran
school believe in #$Transubstantiation as a transformation, but deny the
necessary role of the #$Priest as an agent of this transformation.") 
