(#$comment #$NoteAboutSuccessCriterionSlots "An instance of #$SuccessCriterionSlot is a #$BinaryPredicate
that is used to give a criterion for success for an instance of #$Plan
or an instance of #$Event in a planning context.  More
specifically, instances are used to indicate what must be maintained,
insured, prevented, stopped, achieved, interrupted, upheld, or
thwarted in the #$Plan or #$Event in question, in order for it to
count as a 'success'.  An instance of #$SuccessCriterionSlot does NOT
give the purpose or goal of the instance of #$Plan or #$Event in
question, nor does it say anything explicitly about that goal or
purpose, if it exists (for identifying planning purposes, see
#$PurposeSpecificationSlot).  Also, instances of
#$SuccessCriterionSlot do not state necessary conditions for instances
of #$Plan and #$Event, but only criteria for success: an instance
of #$Plan may be executed, or an instance of #$Situation may 'happen',
even though not all of the success criteria are met.  It does seem to
be the case that known preconditions for an #$Event instance would
count as success criteria, and success criteria in general may be
considered necessary conditions for 'authorities' believing that a
plan's execution was 'successful'.") 
