(#$comment #$PolarCoordinateSystem "The collection of polar coordinate systems, a specialization of #$CoordinateSystem.  Each #$PolarCoordinateSystem has its own measure predicates, for example: #$latitude and #$longitude in the case of the #$GeodeticCoordinateSystem.  In a #$PolarCoordinateSystem SYS for a two- or three-dimensional space, the radial distance coordinates of #$SpatialThings are expressed using a radial distance predicate PRED -- that is, a binary predicate such that (#$radialDistancePredicate PRED SYS) -- in assertions of the form (PRED THING DISTANCE).  Similarly, the azimuth angle coordinates of #$SpatialThings are expressed using a predicate PRED2, such that (#$azimuthAnglePredicate PRED2 SYS), in assertions of the form (PRED2 THING ANGULAR-DISTANCE).  For example, the #$azimuthAnglePredicate of the #$GeodeticCoordinateSystem is #$longitude.  In a #$PolarCoordinateSystem for a three-dimensional space, altitude angle coordinates are expressed using a predicate PRED3, such that (#$altitudeAnglePredicate PRED3 SYS), in assertions of the form (PRED3 THING ANGULAR-DISTANCE).  The predicate #$latitude, for example, is the #$altitudeAnglePredicate of the #$GeodeticCoordinateSystem.  (Note that a particular #$PolarCoordinateSystem might fail to have, say, a radial distance predicate, if defining one would serve no practical purpose.)  See also the comment for #$coordinateValuePredicate.") 
