(#$comment #$CitySectionFn "(#$CitySectionFn ST(1)...ST(N)) denotes the unique
urban region (if any) that is completely bounded by parts of 
each of the streets ST(1) - ST(N).  The relative order (but 
not the absolute position) of the arguments here is important: 
it should be physically possible to circle completely the 
bounded area once in a generally clockwise fashion by starting 
along ST(1) and turning, successively, onto ST(2), ST(3), ..., 
ST(N), and finally back onto ST(1).  In practice, the number 
of streets N will usually be between 3 and 5 (and the shape 
of the region returned roughly an N-gon), though N can be as 
small as 1 if ST(1) is a loop.  To have this function return 
a given street-bounded city section SECTION, one can fill its 
first argument-place with any of the N streets that borders 
SECTION, and then continue to fill in the other argument-places 
according to the order in which one would travel along the 
other N-1 streets if one were circling SECTION in a generally
clockwise fashion (i.e. by making mostly right-hand turns).  
See also #$CityGridSectionFn and #$CityBlockFn.") 
