(#$comment #$NoteAboutQuantityChangePredicates "A family
of predicates is available to talk about net increases and decreases
of quantities over time.  (#$increasesCausallyOverPeriod EVENT THING
SLOT PERIOD) means that EVENT causes the value of the predicate SLOT
applied to THING to be greater immediately after (#$STIF q.v.) PERIOD
than it was immediately before (#$STIB q.v.)  PERIOD.  If PERIOD
starts and ends when EVENT does, see #$increasesImmediatelyCausally.
See #$increasesCausally to avoid precisely stating the period of
increase.  If one wishes to identify a time period or event of a
quantity's increase without implicating a causal relationship, see
#$increasesImmediately.  See the decrease predicates
(#$decreasesCausallyOverPeriod, #$decreasesImmediatelyCausally,
#$decreasesCausally, #$decreasesImmediately) which parallel the scheme mapped out above.  To talk about quantities remaining the same see a set of predicates beginning with the substring 'remains'.  For predicates allowing more expressiveness than simple net increase or decrease, see #$quantityChangeFunction, #$quantityInfluenceFunction, #$quantityInfluenceFunctionStrength.  The later two are particularly noteworthy since they allow statements about factors which *influence* dynamical quantities and do not necessitate actual change in said quantities.") 
