(#$comment #$ExceptionsAndOldConclusionsNote "When an
exception to a rule is asserted into the KB, it prevents
the rule from firing in the future in the cases covered
by the exception. It does NOT go back and remove old
conclusions that have previously been deduced from the
rule. If you think that there are already derived assertions
in the KB that should be 'covered' by your exception,
look at the conclusions deduced from the rule and Blast
any that shouldn't be there. Note: if you assert a 
forward-propogating rule and immediately afterwards
assert an exception to it, the inference engine may
still have deduced conclusions from the rule 
that violate the exceptions. Blast them. TOK 9/97") 
