(#$comment #$StateOfDiplomaticTension "This attribute indicates the degree of diplomatic tension between members of #$GeopoliticalEntity and other types of international agents.  For instance, the state of diplomatic tension between the U.S. and Iran was very high in 1998, that is, in CycL: (#$holdsIn  (#$YearFn 1998) (#$statusWithAgent-Gradient #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$Iran  #$StateOfDiplomaticTension #$VeryHigh)).  In contrast, relations with Canada were  very good that year: (#$holdsIn (#$YearFn 1998) (#$statusWithAgent-Gradient  #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$Canada #$StateOfDiplomaticTension #$VeryLow)).  This attribute  is useful for representing the general state of diplomatic relations between international agents.") 
(#$comment #$nonDeliberateActors "(#$nonDeliberateActors ACT ACTR) means that ACTR has a #$Role in the #$Event ACT but is not acting deliberately.  Examples: (1) President #$JohnKennedy was a #$nonDeliberateActors in his assassination; (2) a person is a #$nonDeliberateActors in his/her own autonomic bodily functioning (e.g., heart beating, digesting); (3) Mount #$Vesuvius-Volcano was a #$nonDeliberateActors in the event of its eruption that destroyed Pompeii.  #$nonDeliberateActors is a negative specification of the role an actor has in an event; the role of particular #$nonDeliberateActors might be further specified positively, e.g., with #$bodilyActedOn or #$bodilyDoer.") 
