(#$comment #$KnowledgeFragmentZ7Mt "To develop trade and economic ties with other countries, states usually adopt policies to facilitate such relations, such as eliminating protectionist measures, reducing trade barriers such as tariffs/levies, granting aid/concessions, and establishing bilateral/multilateral agreements to promote cooperation and regulate interaction.  Trade/economic ties foster and strengthen cooperative relations between countries and provide mutual economic benefits.  States also use trade/economic ties as leverage to coerce or influence other countries - e.g., by cutting off aid or imposing or threatening to impose economic sanctions.  Although states seldom sanction their allies (except in cases of serious trade or other foreign policy disputes, hostile economic actions, or egregious violations of international laws or norms), they often impose economic sanctions on adversaries - for example, to coerce/influence an adversary or to retaliate against/punish a threatening or hostile act or a violation of international law or norms.Z7) To develop trade and economic ties with other countries, states usually adopt policies to facilitate such relations, such as eliminating protectionist measures, reducing trade barriers such as tariffs/levies, granting aid/concessions, and establishing bilateral/multilateral agreements to promote cooperation and regulate interaction.  Trade/economic ties foster and strengthen cooperative relations between countries and provide mutual economic benefits.  States also use trade/economic ties as leverage to coerce or influence other countries - e.g., by cutting off aid or imposing or threatening to impose economic sanctions.  Although states seldom sanction their allies (except in cases of serious trade or other foreign policy disputes, hostile economic actions, or egregious violations of international laws or norms), they often impose economic sanctions on adversaries - for example, to coerce/influence an adversary or to retaliate against/punish a threatening or hostile act or a violation of international law or norms.") 
