(#$comment (#$implies (#$and (#$symptomOfMalfunction ?MALFUNCTION ?MALFUNCTION2) (#$symptomOfMalfunction ?SYMPTOM ?MALFUNCTION)) (#$likelihood (#$symptomOfMalfunction ?SYMPTOM ?MALFUNCTION2) #$MediumToVeryHigh)) "There is some reason to think that symptomhood is not fully transitive.  For instance, if there is some long chain of symptoms (A is a symptom of B, B is a symptom of C, etc.) it may seem unintuitive to assert that the first symptom is a symptom of the last item on the list.  For instance, if we could establish a symptom link from moist upper lip to AIDS through four or five intermediary conditions, would we really want to say that moist upper lip is a symptom of AIDS?  We might think that there is genuine symptomhood in this example if we think that symptomhood has to do largely with whether or not the condition increases the likelihood (and is a condition caused by the ailment) of the condition for which it is alleged to be a symptom.  However, this remains potentially problematic.  If events are dependent, it is not the case that increasing likelihood is transitive.  Events of type A may make events of type B more likely and events of type B may make events of type C more likely but it could be false that events of type A make events of type C more likely.  Given these issues, it was thought best to assume only that A's symptomhood for B suggests an increased likelihood that A is a symptom of C if B is a symptom of C.") 
