(#$salientAssertions #$temporallyCooriginating (#$implies (#$and (#$simultaneousWith ?P1 ?P2) (#$startingPoint ?E1 ?P1) (#$startingPoint ?E2 ?P2)) (#$temporallyCooriginating ?E1 ?E2))) 
(#$cyclistNotes (#$implies (#$and (#$genlMt ?MT #$VulnerabilityMt) (#$ist ?MT ?PROP) (#$ist ?MT (#$isa ?ROLE #$ActorSlot)) (#$ist ?MT (#$istIncreasesSlotValue ?PROP (?ROLE ?EVTTYPE ?AGENT) #$likelihood))) (#$ist ?MT (#$increasedChanceForRoleBecause ?AGENT ?EVTTYPE ?ROLE ?PROP))) "Yes, all of the 'ists' are necessary.  This is because we have to
tell Cyc to check whether PROP is true in the same #$Microtheory in
which it is the case that ROLE is defined as such and that PROP's
being true makes it more likely that AGENT will play ROLE in a
situation of type EVTTYPE.  The #$genlMt clause is precautionary in
the sense that the reason why it is there is to make sure that the
checking only gets done in spec-Mts of the context in which this rule
is asserted: otherwise, because of the pan-KB scope of #$ist, the
conclusion could be generated in microtheories satisfying the antecedent
conditions, even if those microtheories did not have access to the
context (i.e. the #$VulnerabilityMt) where the rule is asserted.  If
Keith will give us an #$IndexicalConcept for referencing 'whatever mt
an assertion is in', we could replace the #$genlMt clause with an
#$indexicalReferent clause that does not reference the
#$VulnerabilityMt by name and which would not have to be changed if
the assertion was moved to another microtheory.  As things stand, if
the assertion is moved, the #$genlMt clause will need to be changed to
reference the new mt.") 
